findings.description:
The document says the snapshot works at any commit and does not modify the repository. It also says the snapshot at a slice's fork point is needed after the fact. Measuring a commit other than HEAD means one of three things, and the document picks none:
- checking it out, which modifies the working tree;
- creating a temporary `git worktree`, which writes to `.git/worktrees`;
- exporting via `git archive`, which breaks tools that need the project's config or a virtualenv.

The principle that a dirty tree is "either refused or recorded with an explicit dirty marker" is also left open, and that choice decides whether pipeline-attached snapshots can run at all on an agent's in-progress tree. The adapter contract needs these decisions before the keystone slice.

The document makes "each language a pluggable adapter" for every metric, and requires a second language adapter "proving the contract wasn't Python-shaped." Its own text says language-agnostic tools exist for duplication and line counts. Multi-language analyzers such as lizard also cover cyclomatic complexity and function length across many languages, using one definition. That removes the "adapters must agree on what cyclomatic complexity means" problem the document raises.

Only lint and type counts are truly per-language. Cut the general adapter contract down:
- one shared multi-language tool for complexity, function length and file length;
- one for duplication;
- per-language plug-ins only for lint and type counts.

This also makes the "additional language adapters" slice much smaller.

"An ingest step reads review files into the store as normalized finding records." The 320 store (`src/squadron/metrology/store.py`) writes one JSON file per record with a uuid-based id. Every query is a glob-and-load of the whole directory (`_iter_records`), and nothing enforces uniqueness. The document doesn't say:
- what happens when the same review file is ingested twice;
- what happens when a review is re-run and overwrites the file;
- what happens when historical reviews are bulk-ingested.

Without a natural key (for example review file + `reviewedSha` + finding id), rollups double-count. If each finding is its own record, a few hundred historical reviews produce thousands of files, and every report re-parses all of them, along with every snapshot and audit record. Either store one record per review with the findings inline (as `AuditRun` does), or state the dedup key. Note the store-scan cost as something to validate.
